I think Jim's spot on the money. Clearly we're all grateful to the dev
team for Nethack 3.4.3, it's brilliant and they are brilliant, and
no-one wishes to appear ungrateful. But that doesn't mean attempting to
improve the process (which often manifests itself as something akin to
complaining at first) should be forbidden.

The dev team's secrecy seems contrary to good software development of
any kind, whether open-source or not. No doubt there are extremely
understandable personal, progmatic or historical reasons why this came
to be the case, but as outsiders to the dev team, it isn't easy to see
what those reasons are - we only see the negative consequences:
apparently glacial progress, lack of transaprency which exacerbates
that, a lack of centralised distribution of point releases, resulting
in a culture of everyone compiling their own incompatable mixes of
patches, and frustrated users.

I understand the dev team has a monumental task, the testing and
cross-platform compatability issues they have to face no doubt make any
small change an immeasurably larger and more tedious job than any of us
non-dev-team folks would believe.

But even this issue, the issue of responsiveness, is a direct symptom
of the dev teams secrecy. In a more open development model, there would
be discussion of responsiveness as a trade-off against, say, retaining
such an obsessive level of cross-platform compatability. I'm sure
someone's itch is being scratched there, and that's all well and good,
but it probably isn't the same itch that the majority of the community
of players would have chosen to spend the last three years scratching.
In a more open model, trade-offs like this would have been made
apparent, and either decided the other way, or else the discussion
would have lent more credibility to a fork which chose to focus on
responsiveness and new features instead.

The negatives resulting from the lack of communication seem so
unneccesary, that I find it hard to understand why things wouldn't be
improved by simply communicating the dev teams status from time to
time, or better still, publishing work in progress and benefitting from
the feedback this engenders. Surely lots of us on this list are
software engineers, and surely we are enlightened enough to know by now
that this is now good software gets written?



